<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Safaviyya</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Safaviyya Sufi order, Safaviyya order</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Safaviyya was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> religious order founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safi-al-din-ardabili">Safi al-Din Ardabili</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a> in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> in the late thirteenth or early fourteenth century. Over time it became increasingly militant and Shia in orientation and gained a devoted following among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> tribes known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a>. The order's leaders used this following to found the Safavid state in 1501.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safi-al-din-ardabili">Safi al-Din Ardabili</a>, who lived from 1252 to 1334 and led the order from 1301, followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shafii-school">Shafi'i school</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a>. His standing is attested in two letters of the vizier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-al-din-hamadani">Rashid al-Din Hamadani</a>, one pledging him a yearly gift of foodstuffs and the other telling Rashid al-Din's son, the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a>, to treat the mystic with proper respect. After his death the leadership passed from father to son, first to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadr">Sadr</a> al-Din Musa, then to Khavjeh Ali and Shaykh Ibrahim.</p><p>From the early 1300s the order's leaders showed that they sought political power as well as religious authority, which made the rulers of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> first uneasy and then hostile. By the mid-15th century, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaykh-junayd">Shaykh Junayd</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaykh-haydar">Shaykh Haydar</a>, it had turned to a ghulat form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelver-shiism">Twelver Shi'ism</a>, declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> against the Christians of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and adopted messianic beliefs about its leaders. Three leaders in a row died in battle: Junayd in 1460, Haydar in 1488 and Ali Mirza in 1494. The order survived under Haydar's son Isma'il.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Isma'il, Junayd's grandson, founded the Safavid empire in 1501, proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelver-shiism">Twelver Shi'ism</a> the religion of the state and brought Twelver Shia scholars, largely from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, to turn the order into a Shi'i dynasty. The Safavid kings rested their authority on three claims: that they were divinely appointed to rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, that they represented on earth Muhammad al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>, the Twelfth Imam whose return Twelver Shi'is await, and that they were the moršed-e kāmel, the perfect spiritual guide of the order. In the years just before 1501 the order's propaganda, its da'va, went further, presenting its leader as the Mahdi himself or even a divine incarnation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Safavid order” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Safavid Empire was founded in 1501 by the fourteen-year-old Ismail I, head of the <b>Safaviyya Sufi order</b> based at Ardabil in north-western Iran.</blockquote><a href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
